066375732421875, 0.0090789794921875, -0.0260467529296875, 0.03424072265625, -0.020538330078125, -0.0082550048828125, -0.03961181640625, -0.03472900390625, -0.02069091796875, -0.033294677734375, 0.03857421875, 0.0293731689453125, 0.0200653076171875, 0.00748443603515625, 0.0272064208984375, -0.00307464599609375, 0.00928497314453125, -0.046173095703125, 0.004962921142578125, -0.0005397796630859375, -0.00015842914581298828, 0.0194549560546875, -0.02520751953125, -0.00347900390625, -0.01519012451171875, -0.015838623046875, -0.0107574462890625, 0.0013484954833984375, 0.00608062744140625, 0.024078369140625, -0.004161834716796875, 0.006351470947265625, 0.0014629364013671875, 0.0255584716796875, -0.025787353515625, 0.018646240234375, 0.0008392333984375, 0.008087158203125, 0.00933837890625, -0.031463623046875, -0.0092010498046875, -0.007328033447265625, -0.022735595703125, -0.020111083984375, 0.00015926361083984375, 0.01422882080078125, -0.03125, 0.011505126953125, -0.0011911392211914062, 0.01268768310546875, -0.00824737548828125, 0.0009822845458984375, -0.0106201171875, 0.0128021240234375], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "How level-0 players behave and how they are perceived by higher-level players are central questions in the literature on level-k models of boundedly rational strategic reasoning. In these models, \"level-0 actions\" refer to the choices that a player with no strategic reasoning would make in a game, while \"level-0 beliefs\" are the expectations that other players have about what such a non-strategic player would do. Standard assumptions in level-k models typically posit that level-0 players either randomize uniformly over all available actions or select actions that may be dominated, reflecting naive or non-strategic behavior.\n\nWe address the research question: How do level-0 actions and beliefs manifest in strategic games, and how do they compare to these standard assumptions in level-k models? To investigate this, we use the intra-team communication method developed by Burchardi and Penczynski (2014), which elicits both the actions that teams would choose as level-0 players (level-0 actions) and their beliefs about what a level-0 player would do (level-0 beliefs). This approach allows us to directly observe and compare these two constructs, which are often assumed to coincide but may differ in practice. Burchardi and Penczynski (2014) demonstrated that this method can reveal discrepancies between actual naive choices and higher-level players' expectations in strategic settings.\n\nWe apply this method to two games: the canonical beauty contest game and a variant of the 11-20 game introduced by Goeree et al. (2018). These games were chosen because they are widely studied benchmarks in the literature and differ in the extent to which standard level-k predictions align with observed behavior. In the beauty contest game, we replicate Burchardi and Penczynski (2014)'s finding that elicited level-0 beliefs closely match observed level-0 actions, supporting the standard level-k assumption that level-0 players choose actions in line with naive or non-strategic reasoning, such as picking the midpoint of the action space. In contrast, in the 11-20 game variant\u2014where Goeree et al. (2018) showed that standard level-k models fail to predict actual choices\u2014behavior deviates from these predictions: elicited level-0 beliefs and observed level-0 actions not only diverge from each other, but both also differ from the standard assumption that level-0 players randomize uniformly or choose dominated actions. Specifically, we observe that some participants use heuristics such as choosing salient or focal numbers (e.g., 11 or 20), or avoiding dominated actions, while others expect level-0 players to select extreme values or round numbers, leading to quantitative and qualitative differences between beliefs and actions.\n\nThis divergence suggests that players may use alternative heuristics or focal points\u2014such as selecting endpoints, avoiding dominated choices, or gravitating toward salient numbers\u2014when reasoning about level-0 behavior, providing a complementary explanation for the behavioral patterns documented by Goeree et al. (2018), who highlighted the limitations of standard level-k predictions in certain games.\n\nOur findings highlight the importance of empirically distinguishing between level-0 actions and beliefs, and suggest that standard assumptions about level-0 behavior may not generalize across games. This has broader implications for the modeling of bounded rationality and strategic reasoning, indicating that more flexible or context-dependent specifications of level-0 behavior may be necessary for accurate predictions. Future research could explore the development of game-specific heuristics for level-0 behavior or investigate how these insights can improve the predictive power of behavioral game theory models in applied settings such as mechanism design or policy interventions."}}